From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 06:08:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Origin ledger: removed-owner re-home lookup by exact
 keys; close the replica on a failed read; pin the review's mutants

Answers review 5965756952 (approve with three non-blocking points) on PR #3633.

1. Performance: `_published_elsewhere` ran `key GLOB '*/board/id'` once per removed-owner obligation on every
   projection, a full replica scan each (about 20 ms on 20,000 rows). A first replacement, one pass over all task
   keys, still cost 37 ms on the MacBook's 23,000 (the scan is not index-covered). `_Publications` now reads each
   gateway's `gateway/info` scope list once per projection, only when a removed owner's row asks, and then does
   exact primary-key reads at `profile/board/id` for the gateways that collect that board. Measured on the
   MacBook's live ledger and replica, five projections each: installed 85-92 ms, this head 86-101 ms (no
   measurable cost); the first commit of this PR measured 131-180 ms. A gateway that announces no scope for the
   board is not consulted: the binding is kept and the row stays unknown (never wrongly dropped).
2. Unpinned clauses: tests for a removed owner's archive with evidence (closed_uncompleted), an owner with task
   records but no info row (not removed), the same id and created_at on another board, a record without
   created_at, and a re-home found only through an announced scope. The own-card clause stays untested here: this
   gateway always publishes its own records, so it is always listed; the clause is a guard for a replica without
   them.
3. `_listed_gateways` and the publications reader now run inside the `try` whose `finally` closes the replica
   connection; a test makes the read fail and checks every opened connection is closed.

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_release_survives_owner_offline.py, 19 tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
